  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "The equation ${a}^{2}+8a=48$ can be written in the form $(a+p{)}^{2}=q.$ Find $p+q,$ and solve the equation.",
  "worked_solution": "First, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}^{2}+8a & =48 \\\\ {a}^{2}+2\\cdot 4\\cdot a & =48 \\\\ {a}^{2}+2\\cdot 4\\cdot a+{4}^{2}-{4}^{2} & =48 \\\\ ({a}^{2}+2\\cdot 4\\cdot a+{4}^{2})-16 & =48 \\\\ {(a+4)}^{2}-16 & =48\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we isolate the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(a+4{)}^{2}-16 & =48 \\\\ (a+4{)}^{2} & =64\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $p=4,q=64,$ and $p+q=68.$\n\nNext, we solve the equation using the square-root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(a+4{)}^{2} & =64 \\\\ \\sqrt{(a+4{)}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{64} \\\\ a+4 & =\\pm 8 \\\\ a & =-4\\pm 8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $a=-12$ and $a=4.$",
